// Part II — The guide. Declared mode: how-to.
page howto_add_resource {
  sites = [:manual]
  title = "Add a resource to a package"

  chapter_header "Add a resource to a package" {
    kicker       = "The guide · how-to"
    reading_time = "4 min read"
    updated      = "2026-09-02"
    version      = "config-weave 0.1.0"
  }

  h2 "Goal"

  p <<'PROSE'
Declare a new resource in an existing package and implement its `check` and
`apply` functions in wscript against the host API, so a playbook step can
invoke it. You need a playbook that already has a `pkgs/<name>/package.wcl`.
The example adds a `file_present` resource; substitute your own name, params
and script body.
PROSE

  h2 "1. Declare the resource and its params"

  p <<'PROSE'
Add a `resource` block to `package.wcl`. `script` is a path relative to the
package directory. Declare one `param` block per input the resource accepts.
Every param carries a `description` and a `type`, and is either `required`
or has a `default`. The engine validates each step's properties against
these params, so a step that passes an unknown or mistyped property fails
validation before anything runs.
PROSE

  code wcl {
    filename = "pkgs/core/package.wcl"
    source = <<'SRC'
resource "file_present" {
  description = "Ensure a file exists with the given content"
  script = "resources/file_present.ws"
  concurrency = "parallel"
  param "path"    { description = "Absolute path"  type = "string"  required = true }
  param "content" { description = "File content"   type = "string"  default = "" }
}
SRC
  }

  p <<'PROSE'
`concurrency` names the class the scheduler uses for this resource. The
classes and what they allow to run alongside each other are in
[package.wcl](ref_package), and the reasoning is in
[Scheduling and concurrency](guide_scheduling).
PROSE

  h2 "2. Implement check and apply"

  p <<'PROSE'
Write the script at the path you declared. It exports `check(params)` and
`apply(params)`. Import each host module you use with `use`. The `params`
value is a map of the step's properties, with defaults filled in.
PROSE

  code rust {
    filename = "pkgs/core/resources/file_present.ws"
    source = <<'SRC'
use value
use fs
use path

// A missing or non-string property is an error the engine reports against the step.
fn param_str(params: Value, key: string) -> Result[string, string] {
    match params.get(key) {
        Some(v) => match v.as_string() {
            Some(s) => Ok(s),
            None => Err(fmt("property '{}' is not a string", key)),
        },
        None => Err(fmt("missing property '{}'", key)),
    }
}

fn check(params: Value) -> Result[CheckResult, string] {
    let p = param_str(params, "path")?
    if !fs::exists(p) { return Ok(CheckResult::NotConfigured) }
    if fs::read(p)? == param_str(params, "content")? {
        Ok(CheckResult::AlreadyConfigured)
    } else { Ok(CheckResult::NotConfigured) }
}

fn apply(params: Value) -> Result[ApplyResult, string] {
    let p = param_str(params, "path")?
    fs::mkdir(path::parent(p))?
    fs::write(p, param_str(params, "content")?)?
    Ok(ApplyResult::Success)
}
SRC
  }

  callout "The contract" {
    kind = :warning
    body = "`check` must never mutate the machine. `apply` must converge, so that a re-check returns `AlreadyConfigured` even in a fresh process. A resource that breaks either rule passes validation and fails at the second apply, or in the testlab."
  }

  p <<'PROSE'
The exact signatures, and the other variants of `CheckResult` and
`ApplyResult`, are in [Script entry points](ref_scripts). Every fallible
host function returns a `Result`, so propagate errors with `?` and let the
engine report them against the step.
PROSE

  p <<'PROSE'
To have your editor type-check the script against the real host surface, run
`config-weave wscripti` in the playbook directory. It writes `weave.wscripti`
and a `wscript.toml` that the wscript language server reads, so a misspelled
host function is flagged as you type. [config-weave wscripti](cmd_wscripti)
describes the output.
PROSE

  h2 "3. Validate that the script compiles"

  p <<'PROSE'
Run validation. Its final stage compiles every script against the host API,
so a wrong signature, an unknown host function or a type error is a hard
error here.
PROSE

  code console {
    source = <<'SRC'
$ config-weave validate ./my-playbook
ok: playbook 'My Playbook' v0.1.0 — 2 package(s), 1 play(s), 3 step(s)
SRC
  }

  callout "Done" {
    kind = :success
    body = "`config-weave validate` exits 0 with the new resource's script compiled. A step can now name it as `core.file_present`, or as `file_present` from inside the same package's tests."
  }

  h2 "Next steps"

  list {
    li "Invoke the resource from a step and converge it: [Check, then apply](howto_check_apply)."
    li "Prove it converges and stays converged in a disposable instance: [Test a package](howto_test_package)."
    li "The host modules a script can import: [The host API](guide_host_api), with one reference chapter per module starting at [fs module](ref_mod_fs)."
    li "The full `resource` and `param` field list: [package.wcl](ref_package). The background on packages: [Packages, resources and gatherers](guide_packages)."
  }
}
